            <AtypicalElectricalLoads deIcingCables="false" exteriorLighting="false" hotTub="true" heatedGarage="false" portableAirConditioning="false" pool="false" mixedUse="false" totalUnits="0" energyStarUnits="0" evChargingStation="false" outdoorGasAppliances="false" commonSpace="false" elevator="false" fitnessRoom="false" snowmeltSystem="false" unheatedGarage="false" />
            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is currently quite air tight, with a few obvious air leakage areas that we identified together during the blower door test. These current air leakage locations include:



** The open (not directly connected) combustion air supply vents for the pellet stove



** The older aluminum slider window in the basement cold room



** The front entry door



** The older ceiling bathroom fan on the main floor (should be upgraded to a new Energy Star rated unit)



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $ 119 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.6 GJ. As mentioned above, your house is already reasonably air tight, and we discussed the addition of a high efficiency Energy Star Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) unit - that will offset these savings, but will ensure proper ventilation of your home. Please read the Ventilation Sections of this report.



If you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x6 (R20). As per our discussions, due to the exterior siding damage (chimney fire) along the right side of your house, you are considering upgrading the siding on all 4 sides of the main floor. This would be a great time to add another minimum R10 of exterior wall insulation (behind the new siding).



I have modeled the addition of a minimum R10 (eg. 2" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 463 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add a minimum R15 or R20, both your rebate &amp; energy savings will increase.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

Your basement walls are 8" concrete block with some 2x3 strapping (and an estimated R8 fibreglass batt) on the interior surface. As your basement is a "walk-out" at the back, the full height of the back wall is exposed to the outside temperatures. My calculations indicate the rear wall exposed surface area is about 29% of the total perimeter.



When you are adding a minimum R10 wall insulation to 100% of the perimeter of the main floor walls, you may want to carry this same exterior insulation (and new siding) down over the back fully exposed wall of the basement.



Savings: By adding a minimum R10 wall insulation to the exterior of the back basement wall, you could potentially save $ 524 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 7.2 GJ, and qualify for up to a $ 362.50 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade. If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 181.25  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

There are eight (8) are older windows in your house (7 on the main floor plus the aluminum slider in the cold storage room) that could be upgraded in conjunction with your planned exterior wall insulation &amp; siding project. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading these 8 all of these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing these 8 older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 666 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ $2,000 in rebates (8 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.</windows>
                <doors>

The front entry on the main floor is an older wooden door that does not close well - it is also a curved top door that will require a wall opening adjustment to accommodate a new Energy Star rectangular door. I recommend you replace this front door with a new Energy Star steel insulated units with a minimum R value of 5.5, and at least double pane windows (triple is better) with low E coatings and argon gas fill between the panes.



Savings: By replacing this older door with higher efficiency Energy Star rated units (as outlined above), you could potentially save $ 111 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.5 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>

Please ensure you read the Heating System section of this report before reading this section.



This house (designed to use 100% electric heating) was originally built to be very air tight. During that time of construction, ventilation design was usually inadequate and your house only has 1 bathroom fan and a clothes dryer that vent to the outside. When these exhaust fans are not in use, the house depends on natural air leakage through various locations in the building envelope.



Most new high efficiency housing from the 90's (and newer) had early generation Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) systems installed to manage ventilation throughout the house, including an adequate fresh air supply to bedrooms &amp; living areas, as well as exhaust ventilation from bathrooms &amp; living/kitchen areas. If your house was being built today, it would have an HRV installed.



As discussed, in conjunction with your research into a new combination heating &amp; hot water system, it would be good to see how best the heating &amp; ventilation contractor could integrate in an HRV that will also use the existing heating ductwork to distribute the "pre-heated" fresh air throughout the house. I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, there is an estimated increase of $ 134 per year in energy costs, however this will mostly be offset by the estimated $ 119 of annual savings from current "uncontrolled" air leakage - see the previous Air Sealing section of my report. The net additional cost for this required ventilation is only about $15 per year.



There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 50 USG electric hot water tank. As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), we we discussed an option you may want to consider - looking at an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane combination boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. One of the loops from the boiler can provide heat to a fancoil that can distribute the heat throughout the house (just like your current electric furnace does). This same boiler will also replace your electric hot water tank &amp; can provide on-demand, continuous use, high efficiency hot water.



Although I quite like using electricity for heating &amp; hot water in "hydro electric" communities of the NWT (as there are no associated GHG's), I am often asked to illustrate the estimated cost of alternative fuels - as the homeowner looks for cost savings. Most Fort Smith residents are already using an alternative fuel for their primary heating appliance (oil or propane furnace or boiler), so performing this optional calculation for your hot water use is often requested. 



Savings: The savings associated with this upgrade are captured in the previous Heating System upgrade section.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house has an electric forced air furnace as the primary heat source and (based on the current construction &amp; insulation that I evaluated during my visit on April 19) is estimated to use about 28,0000 kWh of electricity per year for the heating of your home. Using the Fort Smith electricity rate listed in the Additional Comments section of this report, this would cost about $7,308 per year !!



There is a pellet stove in the basement that you indicated is used frequently. It vents out the right side of the basement wall &amp; there is also a 3" open combustion air supply pipe that feeds two openings in the hearth floor below the pellet stove. You indicated that you find these 2 openings cold on your feet so they are mostly blocked off.



There is a fan system above the pellet stove to push that heat into the ductwork of the electric furnace for distribution throughout the house, however you indicted it is very loud/noisy so it does not gets used often. The only pellet stove heat going upstairs would primarily rise up through the stirwell. I modeled the savings if you used the pellet stove to heat 100% of the basement - my software calculates about $2,275 of net savings (electricity savings, less the cost of the wood pellets).



As there has been an increase in houses &amp; businesses in Fort Smith using propane as a primary fuel source (regular delivery from Hay River), we discussed an option you may want to consider - looking at an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane combination boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. One of the loops from the boiler can provide heat to a fancoil (similar design to your horizontal, ceiling mounted electric furnace) that can distribute the heat throughout the house. This same boiler will also replace your electric hot water tank &amp; can provide on-demand, continuous use, high efficiency hot water.



Savings: By replacing your the current electric furnace &amp; hot water tank with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing propane combination boiler, you could potentially save $ $5,096 in energy costs (compared to 100% electric heat), decrease your annual energy usage by 14.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,500 from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $ 1,250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



As noted above, your current use of the basement pellet stove is estimated to provide about $2,275 of annual heating savings (compared to all-electric heat) - so using the combination boiler would provide an additional $2,821 of annual savings for the total $5,096 (combined heating &amp; hot water).



NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $8,363 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the main floor wall insulation

     - $   363 for the rear basement wall insulation

     - $2,000 for replacing 8 windows with new eligible units

     - $2,500 for installing an eligible combi boiler upgrade

     - $   500 for a 10% air leakage reduction



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the rear basement wall insulation and the combi boiler, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - ECM domestic water circulation pump - 50% up to $175

     - Programmable Thermostat - 25% up to $175

     - LED Lighting - see section further down.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



VENTILATION



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood fan - does not vent to the outside

        - 1 bathroom fan on the main floor

        - A clothes dryer



I would recommend you replace the bathroom fan with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit. You may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat. As your house could use a little more ventilation, you could even install an automatic timer switch to turn this bathroom fan on/off a few times per day for 20-30 minutes.



As outlined in the previous Air Sealing section of this report, your house is reasonably air tight and is expected to become tighter if you proceed with the upgrade recommendations in this report. If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades outlined in this report), it would have a "full house" Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.



Almost all houses from the era of your house would not have had an HRV and instead would manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality by using exhaust fans (kitchen, bathroom, clothes dryer) and incorporate a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace - that would allow outside air each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan is operating. Your heating and ventilation contractor will help you determine the best solutions (available today) for your house.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the bathroom fan and the clothes dryer - the kitchen range hood only recirculates air inside the house), indicated a pressure drop of only 4.9 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current pellet stove is vented to the outside, without a dedicated &amp; sealed combustion air supply - directly connected to the back of the pellet stove.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed smoke detectors, however did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Fort Smith



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Smith:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.26 per kWh

Oil  - $0.90 per litre 

Propane - $0.73

Wood - $262.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.46 per bag, $299.62 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



